From 078c0093f4ad18c7c8ba51b32b5ce4f69ee82e36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 12:28:48 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 11/44] feat(chexc): assumption imports (proposal 5.2.2)

'@AssumeThrows[E1, E2]' on an import imposes the listed exception types on every
call into that package; the bare form assumes 'Exception'. Capability list
aliases apply (6.1), and assumed requirements participate in capability
parameter inference (6.3) exactly like declared ones.

Pipeline, one stage at a time:
- Parse: the annotation is accepted on an 'import' and only there, and only
  under --enable-checked-exceptions; elsewhere it takes the pre-existing
  "unexpected annotation" path.
- Collect: its exception types are collected in the file's top-level scope,
  which is the scope they are named in.
- PreCheck: resolved by ResolveNames, which otherwise walks only top-level
  declarations and so never reaches an import.
- TypeCheck: validated as 'throws' entries; a misplaced annotation is an error
  rather than silently inert, since an inert annotation reads as a claim being
  honoured.
- CapabilityCheck: a per-file package -> types map, unioned into call demands.
  Union, not append: assumptions only ADD obligations relative to the
  read-as-empty default.

The claim is recorded per FILE, since imports are file-scoped and the file that
wrote the import is the scope of the consumer's trust claim, and registered
under every name the import could denote so 'import p.*', 'import p' and
'import p.decl' all match a callee in package p. Matching is exact: a
subpackage is not covered by its parent's assumption.

Also fixes a null-pointer ICE reachable from this feature: an import is not a
declaration, so AddCurFile never descended into its annotations, leaving the
type nodes without a file; IsLegalAccess then dereferences curFile unguarded
(TypeCheckUtil.cpp:1643, behind a CJC_ASSERT that compiles out in release).
The unguarded deref itself is pre-existing and is recorded in the gaps ledger.

Formatting touches adjacent pre-existing lines: cj.py fmt normalises whole
files, matching this branch's existing convention.

Tests: compiler/CheckedExceptions/assume, 6 cases (basic, bare, alias with
inference, negative, misplacement, package scoping) plus a support package
compiled without the flag. LLT Parser+Sema+CheckedExceptions 3995/4000 (the 5
failures are the red-by-design conversions/adapt groups); ctest 49/49.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
